  "query_preview": "../Friday through Monday/...\nActive weather dominates the long term as we watch a strong \nsystem moving into the panhandle over the weekend. Expecting a \nbreak Thursday night into Friday morning before the panhandle \nbegins to cloud over through the day Friday. The front is expected\nto reach the northeastern gulf coast during this time and \ncontinue inland overnight into Saturday where it will linger until\nit starts to shift southeastward through Sunday morning. This \nsystem is expected to bring more significant precipitation amounts\nthrough the weekend with AR influence. Confidence has increased \nfor higher QPF amounts, with 1 to 2 inches expected through the \npeak of the front Saturday. The heaviest impacts are anticipated \nalong the coast from Yakutat to Sitka, with significant orographic\nenhancement expected in the Fairweather, Chilkat, and Coast \nRanges, likely increasing totals in inland areas such as Juneau, \nPetersburg, and Ketchikan. With the longer duration of this event,\nsome locations may approach 3.5 to 4 inches in the 36 hours from \nFriday afternoon to Monday afternoon.\n\nAdditionally, as the front pushes inland Saturday, the increased \npressure gradient will strengthen winds through the inner channels\nand between Cape Suckling and Cape Fairweather, where a weak \nbarrier jet may form as the low moves further east. Increasing \nthrough Friday afternoon and evening, winds in Lynn Canal are \nexpected to reach strong breezes to near gale force winds through \nFriday evening with the possibility of sustained winds reaching \nlow end gale force as the front makes landfall. The rest of the \nchannels should see south to southeasterly fresh breezes as \nthrough Saturday. Temperatures through this period will persist in\nthe upper 50s and 60s, with potential to see upper 60s further \ninland on Friday, given ample clearing.",
